WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:06.510
Make sure you've got your Power Glove equipped
and a memory card handy.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:10.830
We unlocked a few long-hidden secrets in our
earlier video, 7 Gaming Easter Eggs that Weren't

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:11.980
Discovered for Years.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.490
Well, we dug up five more gaming goodies that
remained hidden for longer than anyone would've

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:16.490
guessed.

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:17.610
Mortal Kombat's Secret Menus

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:22.020
While feeding the Mortal Kombat trilogy all
of our quarters during the early '90s, few

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:26.529
suspected that the arcade versions had a back
door to a secret menu made by series co-creator

00:00:26.529 --> 00:00:27.529
Ed Boon.

00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:30.720
To get in, you had to hit the Block button
on the Player One and Player Two sides in

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:31.720
a specific order.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.510
Then you'd be able to run diagnostic tests,
keep track of how many coins the machine had

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:37.050
taken, and watch each character's ending.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:41.690
The secret menus in Mortal Kombat 3 even allowed
you to unlock hidden features, watch a Fatality

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:45.080
demonstration, or play a secret Galaga-type
game.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:52.989
Donkey Kong's Hidden Initials

00:00:52.989 --> 00:00:57.280
An arcade legend since its debut in 1981,
Donkey Kong was so popular it was ported to

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.940
the Atari 400 and 800 consoles.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.220
Landon Dyer, who worked on the port, blogged
in 2008 that he hid an Easter egg in his version

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.220
of the game.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.860
Over a year later, gamer Don Hodges finally
found it.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:12.530
First, play through the game and reach a new
high score between 33,000 and 33,900.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:15.470
Then kill off your remaining lives, with your
final death by falling.

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:19.330
Set your difficulty to four, and after the
game loads the intro, you'll see Landon Dyer's

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.470
initials on the title screen underneath the
Nintendo copyright.

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:26.210
After 28 years, who knew that Donkey Kong
still had an utterly useless trick up his

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:27.210
hairy sleeve?

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.100
GoldenEye's in-game emulator

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:34.500
Seventeen years after GoldenEye: 007's Nintendo
64 debut, one fan found something hidden deep

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.479
within its code: an in-game emulator with
ten working games.

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:46.610
The emulator includes Sabre Wulf, Atic Atac,
Jetpac, Lunar Jetman, Alien 8, Gun Fright,

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:53.009
Underwurlde, Knight Lore, Pssst, and Cookie—all
titles that GoldenEye's developer, Rare, made

00:01:53.009 --> 00:01:55.840
for the ZX Spectrum console in the 1980s.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:57.759
Serious Sam, zero encounters

00:01:57.759 --> 00:02:01.640
There's a hidden level called "Sacred Yards"
in Serious Sam: The First Encounter, which

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.000
usually requires lots of running-and-gunning.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.799
Years after its release, one player found
that you could skip much of the Yards by exploiting

00:02:07.799 --> 00:02:09.670
bugs and shooting certain objects.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:13.940
That got the interest of YouTuber SolaisYosei,
who contacted the game's lead designer, Roman

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:18.300
Ribaric, for answers.​​ Ribaric suggested
using a level editor to unlock the game's

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.300
secrets.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:23.420
That allowed SolaisYosei to record a perfect
playthrough—14 years after the game launched.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:24.420
And finally...

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:25.940
Super Beanstalk Brothers

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:30.110
After more than 30 years, people are still
finding secrets for the first Super Mario

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:31.780
game—like this one.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:33.360
Pay attention, it's kinda complicated:

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:39.060
In a two-player game, let Mario die at World
1-2, then progress Luigi up to World 5-2.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.030
There, proceed to the second Hammer Brother.

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:43.200
There's a hidden beansprout in one of the
blocks.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.660
Let Luigi die from a hammer throw while climbing
the vine.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:50.060
If done correctly, Mario will spawn at World
1-2 with a vine sprouting next to him.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:53.599
You can use the vine to keep hitting the Buzzy
Beetles near the start of the level, spawning

00:02:53.599 --> 00:02:55.760
1-ups until you reach the maximum number of
extra lives.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:56.760
Weird, right?

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.260
Okay, well, not as weird as this…

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:00.269
"Alright!

00:03:00.269 --> 00:03:01.280
Yay!"

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:06.340
Nothing's as weird as that.

00:03:06.349 --> 00:03:07.980
Thanks for watching!

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:10.640
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.290
And leave us a comment to let us know which
video game Easter egg is your favorite...

